Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.230 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  shortly before the chauvin verdict was  announced hundreds of people had  gathered at the site where george floyd  was murdered  in south minneapolis the memorial known  as george  floyd square honors him and the movement  that he inspired  yesterday before the verdict we went  there we met janelle austin  she's one of the site's caretakers what  makes this  this unique you know it's become a kind  of profound  space where people still pay respect we  started our conversation at the say  their name cemetery  it memorializes black americans killed  by police  the power of the installation is that  there are so many names that we don't  know  and it challenges us to look up their  names  learn their story so that they're not  forgotten the 100 headstones  a place for people to grieve what does  it look like for us to create  public spaces where people whenever they  are experiencing their loss  they can come here and the space is big  enough to hold their grief  we then walked around the corner to the  infamous site where george floyd was  killed  from barricades of barricades dorsal  edge square so on june 1st you came here  and did what you said you  you took it upon yourself to preserve it  yeah and what did that mean when you  said i want to preserve this what were  you doing  i was literally picking up trash and  straightening up flowers this is  originally where  the community had built an offering to  acknowledge where his body had laid  but during the trial as i was watching  i noticed that we got the spot wrong and  so i  actually measured out where it was and  we  relayed where his body had laid with  flowers right there that gives me chills  janelle  that gives me chills because it's  important that we remember the story  correctly  and what is the story you want to make  sure that we remember one  that a man was lynched in our community  two  that it was a city employee on city time  who did it  three that the community came together  and said  not in our backyard and we also  recognize that there are conditions  in the context of our community that led  to this incident  and we want those addressed  now janelle alston explained to us that  the memorials are a former protest  because they are disruptive  and she wants that space to be a place  for people to come and express  themselves  however they want to do that whether  it's through prayer protest  art or leaving memorials to george floyd  there are a lot of those there

